Absolutely. Let's review only homeForm.ejs first before touching any controllers or routes.

From what you've shown so far, it is not yet bug-free. The template assumes prediction always exists:

action="<%= prediction ? '/predictions/' + prediction._id : '/predictions' %>"
If the controller doesn't pass prediction, EJS throws:

ReferenceError: prediction is not defined
There are two ways to fix this.
✅ Option 1 (The one I recommend)
Always pass:

prediction: null
from every controller that renders homeForm.ejs.

Then your current EJS works perfectly.

✅ Option 2 (Makes the template more defensive)
If you want the template to survive even when a controller forgets to pass prediction, 
define a local fallback at the top of the file.

Immediately after the header include, add:

<%
const predictionData = typeof prediction !== "undefined" ? prediction : null;
%>
Then change the form to use predictionData:

<form
action="<%= predictionData ? '/predictions/' + predictionData._id : '/predictions' %>"
method="POST">
and every value becomes:

value="<%= predictionData ? predictionData.country : '' %>"
instead of

value="<%= prediction ? prediction.country : '' %>"
This prevents the ReferenceError even if a controller forgets to pass the variable.

Everything else in homeForm.ejs
