For a <select>, you don't use a value attribute on the <select> element itself. 
Instead, you mark the matching <option> as selected.

Here's the correct version for your form:

<select class="form-control mb-3" name="status">

    <option value="Pending"
        <%= prediction && prediction.status === "Pending" ? "selected" : "" %>>
        Pending
    </option>

    <option value="Won"
        <%= prediction && prediction.status === "Won" ? "selected" : "" %>>
        Won
    </option>

    <option value="Lost"
        <%= prediction && prediction.status === "Lost" ? "selected" : "" %>>
        Lost
    </option>

    <option value="Void"
        <%= prediction && prediction.status === "Void" ? "selected" : "" %>>
        Void
    </option>

</select>
How it behaves
Creating a new prediction (prediction is null)
None of the conditions are true, so the browser automatically selects the first option:

Pending
which matches your schema default:

status: {
    type: String,
    enum: ["Pending", "Won", "Lost", "Void"],
    default: "Pending"
}
Editing a prediction
If the prediction has:

status: "Won"
then the rendered HTML becomes:

<option value="Won" selected>Won</option>
and Won appears selected in the dropdown.

This is the standard pattern
Notice you've already used the exact same technique for isPremium:

<option value="true"
    <%= prediction && prediction.isPremium ? "selected" : "" %>>
    Premium Prediction
</option>
and for cardNumber.

So your status dropdown should follow the same pattern for consistency.


